      "media": [
        {
          "id": "image-1",
          "kind": "image",
          "url": "https://cdn1.telesco.pe/file/jTl0QHCvq8uO8ldShQJAF2HBV6RG23uMdlyoyIWyrLBSMqebyPKiToEA4_QQzN74vgH8m9qZ0W7dL-1H_x9So4wauFplSVVM0kAYwl8_YAT3Yx6EOk1siL54FBX0fqy0kpgnmuzK4hYLqBS4v8USKF1_SPNVhiDf6RvVgxnlb0UJG9lne0rBA-xD0o8Az6DmCC0hu3bUudVoP2WXRcRMPfb605KcbNqn2KOa1YnphNq9wfhZbKDNxn0MxJgTJyvlJEoe-lZk5xwRMyRUiklJveNMLIs0HhnBx0JWBn9snBBbm8xfrfTML2sbYvGkJ5kAN2-Ug5ENbZwTHFrTbx3VVw.jpg"
        }
      ],
      "hasMediaInOriginal": true,
      "fallbackReason": null,
      "text": "🎙 Remarks and answers to media questions by Russia’s Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov following the G20 Foreign Ministers’ Meeting (Atlanta, October 30, 2026)\n\n💬 Sergey Lavrov: We support the US Presidency’s firm commitment to depoliticising discussions and focusing on the issues originally agreed upon as the #G20’s agenda.\n\nThis year’s meeting is, of course, part of the preparations for the Miami Summit, which will take place in December.\n\nThe main topics were the impact of geopolitics on economic processes (an important issue), energy, food and supply chains – in other words, everything affected by unlawful sanctions and Western policies.\n\nTo our American hosts’ credit, they do not shy away from discussing these matters and listen attentively. I do not know to what extent this will be reflected in the final agreed documents, but the approach is right: ☝️ depoliticise discussions and focus on real problems.\n\nAnother point that US Secretary of State Marco Rubio particularly emphasised when discussing preparations for the Miami Summit was that twenty countries, the European Union and the African Union are represented here. Given the influence of #G20 members on the global economy and their combined economic weight, however, the decisions we take will affect virtually every country. We must always bear this in mind.\n\n🤝 Let me reiterate that we welcome this firm commitment to dialogue.\n\nWork on the Miami Summit’s outcome documents has, in fact, already begun. There will be many difficult issues, since interests are at stake across all these areas, including energy and food security. The impact of conflicts, including the war unleashed against us by the West through the Ukrainian regime, remains to be discussed. &lt;...&gt;\n\n***\n\nOther key points:\n\n• Under the pragmatic US Presidency of the #G20, we believe there is a chance of reaching agreements that will be implemented.\n\n• We see no need to add another European voice [Poland] to the G20, especially one that is far from constructive. You know the positions Poland regularly voices at international events. The G20 must reflect actual trends in the global economy, where countries of the Global South and East are gaining prominence.\n\n• We spoke [with US Secretary of State Marco Rubio] about certain aspects of our bilateral relations and cooperation in multilateral forums, including the #UN, the #G20 and #APEC, that will host a Summit shortly in Shenzhen (China).\n\n• Despite all our differences, the US willingness and interest in pursuing dialogue at various levels and through a range of other channels can only be welcomed. This is a good lesson for our European colleagues, who persist in their arrogant boycott.\n\n• Everyone can see that the Europeans want to squeeze their way into the negotiating process [on Ukraine]. Clearly, they want to avoid leaving these negotiations solely to Russia and the US at all costs. What matters is not what the Europeans want, but what added value they can actually bring.\n\n• As for the talks in Abu Dhabi, I can say that they will take place. Our position is well known. It is unclear, however, what the Ukrainians intend to bring to the negotiating table.\n\n👉 As President Vladimir Putin has said, if Zelensky wants a meeting, he can come to Moscow. We guarantee his safety and security, which, as far as I understand, cannot be guaranteed in Kiev.\n\nRead in full",
